# Append to /etc/fstab: the writable footage partition (3rd partition on the
# card; OS partition goes read-only via `raspi-config` > Performance > Overlay FS).
# cli/dashberry-install appends this automatically (by PARTUUID) when it builds
# the card — this snippet stays as the manual-install reference.
# nodelalloc + data=ordered + commit=1: an append allocates its blocks at
# write() time, which puts it in the journal transaction the 1 s commit is
# about to close, and ordered mode writes the data blocks ahead of that
# commit — so a hard power cut (every ignition-off, and an impact that kills
# 12 V) loses ~1-2 s of the open segment. nodelalloc is LOAD-BEARING in that
# chain: with ext4's default delayed allocation, appended pages sit outside
# every transaction until writeback (30 s dirty_expire, 5 s flusher pass),
# commit=1 has nothing to order against, and a power cut leaves the open
# segment as it was at the last writeback — 0 BYTES on the card for a tail
# younger than ~30 s, for all that filesink wrote it unbuffered. Closed
# segments do not depend on this line: each recorder fsyncs a segment (and
# the directory entry) the moment splitmuxsink closes it. This line is about
# the segment being written when the 12 V dies, which is the one with the
# impact on it.
#
# What it CANNOT do is make a partially-filled 4 KB page whole. The commit
# flushes that page with its tail zeroed, and the bytes that complete it are
# written afterwards; if power goes before they land, the zeros stay. That is
# survivable only because segments are MPEG-TS — the loss costs the frames it
# covers, and the reader resyncs on the next 188-byte packet. The same holes
# in fragmented MP4 destroyed a whole GOP each AND, once one landed on the
# header, hid the entire file (crash tail, 2026-08-07).
/dev/mmcblk0p3  /data  ext4  defaults,noatime,nodelalloc,data=ordered,commit=1,nofail  0 2
